\description{
\code{qes_harmonize()} builds one data frame from several studies, with one
column per harmonized variable ("target") and one row per respondent. It
does only what the reviewed harmonization spec says: for each study and
target, one question of that study, its codes mapped one by one to the
target's levels, and a reason for every missing value. Nothing is matched
by name or guessed, and a code the spec does not map is an error, never
passed through. \code{\link[=qes_spec]{qes_spec()}} shows the spec: which studies have which
target, how comparable each study's question is, and the exact mapping.
}
\section{Experimental}{

The engine and its spec are experimental: targets, grades and mappings are
reviewed study by study and may still change. A crosswalk row is applied
only once a reviewer has signed it off (status \code{"stable"}); rows checked
against the files but not yet signed off (status \code{"review"} or \code{"draft"})
are applied only with \code{include_draft = TRUE}, and a message says so. In
spec 4.0.0 most rows are signed off, after an automated double review
against the original files and documents (not a human review; the
crosswalk's \code{reviewed_by} says so). The rows of \code{qes1998},
\code{qes2007_panel}, \code{qes2012_panel} and the CROP polls stay in review until
their recommended weights are reviewed, and the \code{qes2014} gender row until
a second reviewer confirms its grade; by default their values are missing
(reason \code{not_reviewed}), and \code{qes_spec("crosswalk")$review_note} says why
each is held. When no cell of the result is applied for that
reason, a warning of class \code{qesR_warning_all_unreviewed} gives the number
of values left \code{NA} and names \code{include_draft = TRUE}; otherwise a message
counts the cells (study and target) and values left out.

Every result records the spec version and content hash
(\code{attr(, "qes_spec")}). The same spec version and hash, the same pinned
files and the same qesR version give the same data values and the same
study and cell provenance; only the \code{created} time of the spec-level
provenance (\code{qes_provenance(x, level = "spec")}) differs between calls. To keep a spec
fixed, copy \verb{inst/extdata/harmonize/} of a qesR release and pass its path
as \code{spec}.
}

\section{Targets and comparability}{

A target is one question stimulus: a different wording, scale, timing or
format makes another target (vote intention and reported vote, the
"independent country" and "sovereign country" referendum questions, the
four-point and 0-10 interest scales are all separate targets, never
pooled). Each study's question gets a grade against the target's anchor
question:
\itemize{
\item \code{identical}: the same question, options and format;
\item \code{comparable}: the same stimulus, with differences (option order, whether
"don't know" is offered, minor parties listed) not expected to move the
shares of the common answers;
\item \code{approximate}: the same construct, in a format or with a filter expected
to move them.
}

\code{min_grade} keeps only cells at or above a grade; the others become \code{NA}
with reason \code{below_grade}. By default approximate cells are included, and
a message lists them.

Answer options that a study's question did not offer (the Conservative
party before 2022, say) are \emph{structural zeros}: their share in that study
is 0 because nobody could choose them, not because nobody supported them.
The factor levels are the same in every study, so these levels are there
with no respondent; \code{qes_provenance(x, level = "cell")$levels_not_offered}
lists them, and printing the result names them.
}

\section{Missing values}{

Every missing value has a reason, in this order (the levels of the \code{<target>__na} columns and the \code{n_<reason>} columns of \code{qes_provenance(x, level = "cell")}); generated from the catalog vocabulary:
\describe{
\item{\code{dk}}{Don't know.}
\item{\code{refused}}{Refused.}
\item{\code{dk_refused}}{Don't know or refused (one code).}
\item{\code{no_answer}}{No answer (item nonresponse).}
\item{\code{not_selected}}{Not selected (multiple choice).}
\item{\code{inapplicable}}{Inapplicable (routed out).}
\item{\code{not_voted}}{Did not vote.}
\item{\code{spoiled}}{Spoiled ballot.}
\item{\code{ineligible}}{Not eligible to vote.}
\item{\code{not_registered}}{Not on the list of electors.}
\item{\code{not_in_wave}}{Not in this wave.}
\item{\code{not_mappable}}{Source category straddles target levels.}
\item{\code{sysmis}}{System missing.}
\item{\code{not_asked}}{Not asked in this study.}
\item{\code{not_reviewed}}{Crosswalk row not yet signed off by a reviewer.}
\item{\code{below_grade}}{Below the requested grade.}
\item{\code{unmapped}}{Code not mapped.}
}
\code{not_asked} means the study has no question for the target; \code{not_reviewed} that it has one, whose crosswalk row is not yet signed off by a reviewer (applied with \code{include_draft = TRUE}). \code{missing = "reasons"} adds a factor column \code{<target>__na} with the reason of each missing value.
}

\section{Data}{

By default each study is read from its pinned original file, checked by
md5, as \code{\link[=get_qes]{get_qes()}} reads it. \code{data} gives the frames instead, for
example ones you already read: \code{data = list(qes2018 = get_qes("qes2018"))}.
Their origin cannot be verified, so a warning says so and
\code{qes_provenance(x)$md5_verified} is \code{FALSE}. Give them as \code{\link[=get_qes]{get_qes()}}
returns them (labelled codes, not factors). The synthetic study
\code{"qes_demo"} is harmonized with the rows of \code{qes2014}, whose variables it
copies; targets it has no question for are \code{not_asked}.
}

\section{Waves}{

Each study has one or more waves (a post-election survey, the waves of a
panel, the campaign-period and post-election surveys of 2022), declared
in the spec with the rule that says who took part in each: a
disposition or date variable, never an answer. A question belongs to the
wave that asked it, so a respondent outside that wave is \code{NA} with reason
\code{not_in_wave}. The respondent layout has one row per respondent, and
\code{waves} lists the waves each respondent took part in. The long layout
(\code{layout = "long"}) has one row per respondent and wave (a respondent in
no wave keeps one row, with \code{wave} \code{NA}): a value sits on the row of the
wave that asked it and the respondent's other rows are \code{not_in_wave},
except the time-invariant targets (year and month of birth), which are
repeated on each of the respondent's rows.

Pooled polls (the monthly CROP polls of 2007-2010) have one wave per
poll: each respondent belongs to one poll, whose questions the spec
maps once for all the polls. In the same way, a question that does not
change over a panel (gender, education, mother tongue) is mapped once
for all its waves when each respondent answered it in whichever wave
they took part (the 2007 panel). Each poll refers to the next general
election, which \code{election_date} gives row by row, \code{year} is the year the
poll began, and its weight is normalized within the poll. \code{stratum}
gives the independent sample a respondent was drawn in, where a study
pools several: for pooled polls the poll's wave name (as in \code{waves});
for the 1998 panel the polling firm's code in the file, \code{firme_post}
(\code{"1"} = CREATEC, \code{"2"} = CROP; each firm interviewed francophones
only). \code{\link[=qes_design]{qes_design()}} uses it.

\code{interview_date} is the wave's interview date where the file has one
(\code{NA} otherwise; the fieldwork dates of each wave are in
\code{qes_spec("spec")$tables$waves}), and \code{days_to_election} the number of
days from it to the election (negative after the election).
\code{survey_mode} is \code{"web"}, \code{"phone"} or \code{"mixed"}: the wave's mode, or,
where it varies by respondent (the 2018 panel's first wave), the
respondent's own.
}

\section{Weights}{

Each wave has at most one recommended weight in the spec's registry, and
never one calibrated on the vote. The respondent layout has \code{weight_pre}
and \code{weight_post}, the recommended weights of the respondent's
pre-election and post-election waves (\code{NA} outside them), with the name
of the source variable in \code{weight_pre_var} and \code{weight_post_var}; the
long layout has \code{weight} and \code{weight_var}. \code{weights = "normalized"}
(default) divides each wave's weight by its mean over the wave's members
with a weight, so it has mean 1 in each study and wave; this fixes the
scale only (it does not give studies equal shares when pooled: see
\code{\link[=qes_design]{qes_design()}}). \code{weights = "raw"} keeps the weights as deposited. A
weight whose method is not documented yet (registry status
\code{needs_review}) is \code{NA}, with a message, until it is reviewed; the raw
variables are still in the data read by \code{\link[=get_qes]{get_qes()}}, to join by
\code{source_row}.

A pre-election question (vote intention) is weighted with \code{weight_pre}
and a post-election one (reported vote) with \code{weight_post}.
\code{attr(, "qes_weight_guide")} gives, for each target and study, the wave
that asked it and the weight column and variable that fit it, and a
message says when the targets of a study need different weights. For a
question that covers every wave of a panel (wave \code{"*"}, such as gender
in the 2007 panel), the guide names a weight only when those waves share
it; otherwise \code{weight_column} and \code{weight_var} are \code{NA}, since each
respondent's weight depends on the waves they took part in.
}

\section{Eligibility}{

\code{eligible_voter} says whether the respondent could vote in the study's
election: 18 or older on election day and, where the study asked,
a Canadian citizen. It comes from the age targets of the spec (year and
month of birth, age, age group) and the citizenship target, read
whatever \code{targets} and \code{min_grade} ask for (but, like any target, only
from rows signed off by a reviewer, or with \code{include_draft = TRUE}).
It is \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE} only when the answers settle it: someone born
18 years before the election year whose month of birth is unknown, or
aged 17 when interviewed before the election, is \code{NA}, as is a
respondent whose study has no age question in the spec yet. The 2018
study sampled people aged 16 and over, so some of its respondents are
\code{FALSE}. Its weights target the population aged 16 and over; keeping
only eligible voters does not make them an 18-and-over calibration.
}

\section{Conditions}{

Besides those of every qesR function (see \link{qesR-package}),
\code{qes_harmonize()} raises these classed conditions:
\itemize{
\item errors: \code{qesR_error_unmapped} (a source code the spec does not map,
with \code{unmapped = "error"}; fields \code{study}, \code{target}, \code{codes}, \code{n},
\code{unmapped}), \code{qesR_error_spec} (an invalid spec, or \code{data} that fail the
spec's checks; field \code{problems}, a data frame of the failed rules) and
\code{qesR_error_duplicate_id} (identifier variables that do not identify the
rows of a study uniquely; fields \code{study}, \code{id_vars}, \code{rows});
\item warnings: \code{qesR_warning_all_unreviewed} (no cell applied, see
\code{include_draft}), \code{qesR_warning_unmapped} (codes set to \code{NA} with
\code{unmapped = "warn"}; field \code{unmapped}), \code{qesR_warning_partial} (studies
left out with \code{on_fail = "skip"}; field \code{failed}) and
\code{qesR_warning_unverified_source} (\code{data} not read by qesR from the
pinned files; field \code{study}), and \code{qesR_warning_label_mismatch} and
\code{qesR_warning_universe} (such \code{data} whose value labels differ from
the pinned file's, or whose answers do not fit the question's
universe; fields \code{study}, \code{problems});
\item messages, silenced by \code{quiet = TRUE}: \code{qesR_message_unreviewed_skipped}
(cells left \code{NA} because their rows are not signed off),
\code{qesR_message_unreviewed_cells} (cells that use rows not signed off,
with \code{include_draft = TRUE}), \code{qesR_message_approximate_cells} (cells
graded approximate are included), \code{qesR_message_structural_zeros}
(levels a question did not offer), \code{qesR_message_weight_review}
(recommended weights that need review, left \code{NA}) and
\code{qesR_message_weight_timing} (targets of one study that need different
weights).
}

\code{\link[=qes_design]{qes_design()}} sends \code{qesR_message_design_dropped} (fields \code{study}, \code{n})
when it leaves out rows without the chosen weight.
}

\section{En français}{

\code{qes_harmonize()} (expérimental) construit un seul tableau à partir de
plusieurs études, une colonne par variable harmonisée (« cible ») et une
ligne par répondant, en appliquant uniquement la spécification révisée :
une question par étude et par cible, ses codes appariés un à un aux
niveaux de la cible, et un motif pour chaque valeur manquante. Un code non
apparié est une erreur. Chaque cellule (étude, cible) porte un niveau de
comparabilité (\code{identical}, \code{comparable}, \code{approximate}) ; \code{min_grade}
écarte les cellules sous un niveau donné. Les options qu'une question
n'offrait pas sont des zéros structurels, pas un appui nul. \code{lang = "fr"}
donne les étiquettes des niveaux en français ; les codes sont les mêmes.
\code{targets = "decon"} demande les cibles des colonnes de \code{\link[=get_decon]{get_decon()}}.
La disposition longue (\code{layout = "long"}) donne une ligne par personne et
par vague ; une question invariante d'un panel (genre, scolarité) est
lue dans la vague à laquelle la personne a participé (panel de 2007) ;
les sondages CROP regroupés ont une vague par sondage, et
\code{stratum} donne l'échantillon indépendant d'où vient la personne : pour
les sondages regroupés, le nom de la vague du sondage (comme dans
\code{waves}) ; pour le panel de 1998, le code de la firme dans le fichier,
\code{firme_post} (\code{"1"} = CREATEC, \code{"2"} = CROP). Chaque sondage se rapporte
à l'élection générale suivante, que \code{election_date} donne ligne par
ligne, \code{year} est l'année où il a commencé, et sa pondération est
normalisée à l'intérieur du sondage. Les pondérations recommandées de chaque vague sont dans
\code{weight_pre} et \code{weight_post} (ou \code{weight} en disposition longue),
ramenées à une moyenne de 1 par étude et par vague ; une pondération
non encore documentée vaut NA. \code{attr(, "qes_weight_guide")} donne, pour
chaque cible et étude, la colonne de pondération qui convient ; pour une
question qui couvre toutes les vagues d'un panel (vague \code{"*"}), elle vaut
NA quand ces vagues n'ont pas la même pondération. \code{eligible_voter} indique si la personne
pouvait voter (18 ans le jour du scrutin et, là où l'étude l'a demandé,
citoyenneté canadienne) ; \code{interview_date}, \code{days_to_election} et
\code{survey_mode} décrivent l'entrevue. \code{\link[=qes_design]{qes_design()}} en fait un plan de
sondage. Des résultats portant sur des études différentes se combinent
avec \code{\link[=rbind]{rbind()}} s'ils ont été construits avec la même spécification, les
mêmes \code{lang}, \code{layout}, \code{values}, \code{missing} et \code{weights} et les mêmes
colonnes (sinon, une erreur de classe \code{qesR_error_input}). Une ligne de
correspondance n'est appliquée qu'une fois approuvée par un réviseur
(statut \code{"stable"}) ; \code{include_draft = TRUE} applique aussi les lignes
vérifiées mais pas encore approuvées (statut \code{"review"} ou \code{"draft"}).
Dans la spécification 4.0.0, la plupart des lignes sont approuvées, après
une double révision automatisée sur les fichiers et documents originaux
(et non une révision humaine ; la colonne \code{reviewed_by} le dit) ; les
lignes de \code{qes1998}, \code{qes2007_panel}, \code{qes2012_panel} et des sondages
CROP restent en révision jusqu'à ce que leurs pondérations recommandées
soient révisées, et la ligne gender de \code{qes2014} jusqu'à ce qu'un second
réviseur confirme son niveau. Sans \code{include_draft = TRUE}, leurs valeurs
sont NA (motif \code{not_reviewed}), \code{qes_spec("crosswalk")$review_note} dit
pourquoi chacune est retenue, et un avertissement de classe
\code{qesR_warning_all_unreviewed} le signale quand tout le résultat est NA. Les autres conditions ont des classes (section
\emph{Conditions}) : erreurs \code{qesR_error_unmapped}, \code{qesR_error_spec} et
\code{qesR_error_duplicate_id} (variables d'identification qui n'identifient
pas les lignes de façon unique) ; avertissements \code{qesR_warning_unmapped},
\code{qesR_warning_partial} et \code{qesR_warning_unverified_source} ; messages
\verb{qesR_message_*}, masqués par \code{quiet = TRUE} ; \code{\link[=qes_design]{qes_design()}} envoie
\code{qesR_message_design_dropped} quand il écarte des lignes sans la
pondération choisie. Voir
\code{vignette("fr-reference-harmonisation", package = "qesR")}.
}

\examples{
# the synthetic demonstration study, harmonized with the signed-off
# qes2014 rows (its gender row, still in review, is left NA)
h <- qes_harmonize("qes_demo")
h
table(h$vote_prov_recall, useNA = "ifany")

# why values are missing, and the grade of each cell
h <- qes_harmonize("qes_demo", targets = c("vote", "sov_indep"),
                   missing = "reasons", quiet = TRUE)
table(h$vote_prov_recall__na)
cells <- qes_provenance(h, level = "cell")
cells[, c("study", "target", "source_var", "grade", "n_valid")]

# French labels, same codes
h_fr <- qes_harmonize("qes_demo", targets = "interest_4pt", lang = "fr", quiet = TRUE)
levels(h_fr$interest_4pt)

# weights and eligibility: every question of the demonstration study was
# asked after the election, so weight_post is its weight (mean 1)
h <- qes_harmonize("qes_demo", targets = "sov_indep", quiet = TRUE)
h[1:3, c("study", "waves", "eligible_voter", "sov_indep", "weight_post", "weight_post_var")]
attr(h, "qes_weight_guide")

# one row per respondent and wave
l <- qes_harmonize("qes_demo", targets = "sov_indep", layout = "long", quiet = TRUE)

# the rows still in review, and why each is held
xw <- qes_spec("crosswalk")
head(xw[xw$status == "review", c("study", "target", "review_note")])
table(l$wave, l$wave_timing)
}
